---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import KpiDashboard from '../../components/KpiDashboard.astro';

const pages = [
  'Executive overview',
  'NEST and ROOT visits',
  'Nursery and outplanting',
  'Contracts',
  'Inactive farmers',
  'Staff performance',
  'Impact',
  'Data quality',
];
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="program-kpi"
  facts={[
    ['Where', 'Trees for the Future, Kenya'],
    ['Tools', 'Power BI Desktop, Power Query, DAX'],
    ['Source', 'Salesforce objects fed by TaroWorks field forms'],
  ]}
>
  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Problem</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Kenya field reporting ran on two Excel workbooks. One held a row per farmer and was refreshed by hand. The
        other took pasted form submissions and was recalculated each time.
      </p>
      <p>The aim was one model that refreshes itself and can later take in Uganda and Tanzania.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">What I did</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Six Salesforce queries came in through Power Query: contracts, farms, local groups, two nursery forms and
        outplanting. I reshaped them into 13 tables. Six are lookup tables (project unit, local group, farmer, staff,
        date, collector). Five hold events (NEST, NEST seedlings, ROOT, contract signing, visits). Two hold assumptions
        and the measures. They are joined by 16 relationships and reported through 107 measures.
      </p>
      <p>Most of the work was deciding what to count. Four decisions changed the totals:</p>
      <ul class="list">
        <li>
          A physical visit is counted once per farmer per day. Two forms filled in on the same visit no longer make two
          visits.
        </li>
        <li>
          Tree totals and outplanting completion use each farmer's latest ROOT record. Adding up every past snapshot
          would count the same trees repeatedly.
        </li>
        <li>A farmer's group membership is read from the record in force on the day of the visit.</li>
        <li>Recency is shown next to the date the data was last refreshed, so a stale refresh is visible.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The report has eight pages: {pages.join(', ').toLowerCase()}.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <KpiDashboard />

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Reading the demo</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        This is a web rebuild of the main views from the overview and visits pages, on generated data. Pick a project
        unit to filter everything. The unit chart ranks units by the share of their farmers who have been visited.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Result</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every KPI in the programme's KPI document is implemented as a measure, and the totals were reconciled against
        the live data. The report package passed Power BI's report-format validator with no errors or warnings.
      </p>
      <p>
        The data quality page surfaced problems the workbooks had hidden: records dated in the future, farmers carrying
        more than one "most recent" nursery row, and contract events with no matching farmer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Next steps</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The plan is to extend the model from Kenya to Uganda and Tanzania. Farm boundaries sit in a separate Salesforce
        object from the farm record and still need joining in.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  .list {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    padding-left: 1.1em;
    list-style: square;
  }

  .list ::marker {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
